u}$ decays by taking advantage of the $K^+ightarrow\pi^+ \pi^0$ decay. Unlike traditional searches that utilize fixed-target experiments, this new method leverages a beam of decaying kaons produced from $K^+$ mesons, also created from $K^+ightarrow\pi^+\pi^0$ decays in the same kinematic conditions as the kaons to be studied. This shared environment simplifies the selection process and reduces background interference. A simulated experiment using a beam of 1.5 GeV/c $K^+$ mesons has been conducted to investigate the feasibility of the proposed method by examining $K^+ightarrow\pi^+
